-- A root computes its own values, by what they read (backend.md §15.4,
-- *A root computes its own values*; boundary.md §9.4.6, 1.5). `view`'s
-- block carries its input, `model`, and the kind's `p` reads the fields
-- under one test per set of fields: the two writes that read
-- `model.name` share one, `model.count` has its own, and a value that
-- reads nothing — the title made by a call of constants — is written
-- once, under a test that holds only at mount. The input's `value`,
-- `stateful`, is tested on `model.text` like any value and written by
-- `control`, which compares it with the page (§15.3, *Controlled inputs*,
-- amended 2026-10-08); the kind is not live.
-- `m` writes nothing a value says: it clones, walks, keeps `undefined` in
-- every field and calls `p`. In `ordered`, the `span`'s two attributes
-- read `count` and `name`, whose groups would write them the other way
-- round on a mount, so the two groups merge: an element's attributes are
-- written in source order. In `shouted`, `loud` is used only inside the
-- root, so the root computes it (the `let` rule): first in `p`, under
-- the test of `model.name`, kept in `d0`, and read from there by both
-- writes, whose group tests the same field.
import Html exposing (Html)


type alias Model =
    name : String
    count : Int
    text : String


pub view : Model → Html msg
view model =
    <div class={model.name}>
        <p title={String.fromInt 7}>{model.count}</p>
        <b>{model.name}</b>
        <input value={model.text} />
    </div>


pub ordered : Model → Html msg
ordered model =
    <div class={model.name}>
        <span title={String.fromInt model.count} class={model.name}>x</span>
    </div>


pub shouted : Model → Html msg
shouted model =
    loud = String.toUpper model.name
    <p title={loud}>{loud}</p>
